<clickhouse>
    <!-- Turns off the QUERY-thread sampling profiler.

         This is HALF the fix and does little on its own. Its counterpart is
         global_profiler_real_time_period_ns in config.d/log-retention.xml,
         which covers background threads and accounts for 99.6% of the Real
         samples here. Both are needed; either alone leaves most of trace_log's
         volume in place. Measured immediately after this file went live and
         before the server setting did: 42 query-thread samples against 11,046
         background ones in three minutes.

         Why the two live in different files: these are user-PROFILE settings,
         which ClickHouse reads only from users.xml / users.d, while
         global_profiler_* are SERVER settings, which it reads only from the
         config. Each is a silent no-op in the other's file, parsing cleanly and
         doing nothing. Hence the separate mount in docker-compose.yml.

         Measured composition of trace_log on mainnet 2026-08-03, over ~4 days:

           Real         22,062,374   59.2%   query profiler, 1s per thread
           Memory        7,745,639   20.8%   memory profiler (KEPT, see below)
           MemoryPeak    7,349,165   19.7%   memory profiler (KEPT)
           CPU              91,755    0.2%   query profiler, CPU clock

         Those Real rows are overwhelmingly background threads rather than
         queries, which is why this file alone was not enough. Neither source
         had ever been read: across the entire query_log history the only
         statements touching system.trace_log were a TTL inspection and the
         TRUNCATE from the 2026-07-30 cleanup, plus the queries from this
         investigation. Nothing in the application or the runbooks consumes it.
         Sampling also costs CPU in signal handling and stack unwinding, so
         switching both off is a small throughput win as well as ~1.1 GiB.

         The MEMORY half is deliberately left ON. This container runs under a
         4 GiB mem_limit that it has already exceeded once mid-mutation (see
         config.d/log-retention.xml), and memory traces are precisely what
         explains an OOM after the fact. Trading ~750 MiB for that answer is
         the wrong trade, so memory_profiler_step is untouched at its 4 MiB
         default.

         To profile a slow query later, re-enable per session rather than
         reverting this file:
           SET query_profiler_real_time_period_ns = 1000000000;
         Setting either knob to 0 disables that sampler. -->
    <profiles>
        <default>
            <query_profiler_real_time_period_ns>0</query_profiler_real_time_period_ns>
            <query_profiler_cpu_time_period_ns>0</query_profiler_cpu_time_period_ns>
        </default>
    </profiles>
</clickhouse>
